import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import VivCell from '../components/VivCell.jsx';
import { cells } from '../data/vivTourCells.mjs';
---

<Base
  title="Language tour · Viv"
  description="A tour of Viv with an example you can run for each feature: numbers, control flow, functions, arrays, plots, linear algebra, lambdas, tests, narration, parallel loops, records."
  eyebrow="Language tour"
  heading="Hello Viv!">
  <p class="lede">
    Every example below runs in your browser through the same wasm compiler the
    <a href="/workbench">workbench</a> uses. Running a cell runs it together with every cell
    above it, starting fresh each time. An edit to one cell changes the results of every cell below
    it, and nothing is left over from an earlier run.
  </p>

  <h2 id="numbers">Numbers</h2>
  <p>
    A number is a 64-bit float: <code>10</code> means <code>10.0</code>, and division never
    truncates. For counters and indices there is <code>Int</code>, a 64-bit integer declared
    with <code>n: Int</code>. <code>print</code> gives the
    bare value; <code>show</code> labels a variable with its name and prints any other
    expression bare:
  </p>
  <div class="cellhost"><VivCell client:load idx={0} src={cells[0]} /></div>

  <h2 id="control-flow">Control flow</h2>
  <p>
    Three forms: <code>if/else</code>, <code>while</code>, and the counted loop
    <code>for i to n</code>, which runs <code>i</code> from 0 to n−1. <code>break</code> and
    <code>continue</code> work as in C. Statements end at the newline (no semicolons), and a line
    continues automatically inside parentheses or brackets:
  </p>
  <div class="cellhost"><VivCell client:load idx={1} src={cells[1]} /></div>

  <h2 id="functions">Functions</h2>
  <p>
    <code>def</code> defines a function, <code>return</code> returns, and a function can call itself. A bare string as
    the first statement is a docstring. Parameter and return types (<code>Num</code>,
    <code>Arr</code>) are optional annotations, checked when the program compiles.
  </p>
  <p>
    A 64-bit float (a double)
    holds every integer up to 2<sup>53</sup> exactly, so counting down from <code>10.0</code> by
    <code>1.0</code> lands <em>on</em> <code>1.0</code>, not near it: <code>n &lt;= 1.0</code> is an
    exact test. <code>fact(20.0)</code> is exact as well: 20! is past 2<sup>53</sup>, but it has eighteen
    factors of 2, so what is left fits in a double's 53 bits. What a double cannot hold is
    a fraction like <code>0.1</code>. That is where <code>==</code> fails and a tolerance belongs:
    <code>0.1 + 0.2</code> misses <code>0.3</code> by 5.6 × 10<sup>−17</sup>, and
    <code>assert_near</code> is how a Viv program says “equal, to within rounding.”
  </p>
  <p>
    After you run this cell, look under its output. The interpreter records every user-function call, so
    each cell carries a collapsible <strong>call graph</strong> — open here so you can see it.
    <code>fact</code> calling itself is the self-loop with its count, and <em>replay</em> animates
    the recorded call sequence. In later cells the graph stays folded behind the "call graph" line
    until you want it:
  </p>
  <div class="cellhost"><VivCell client:load idx={2} src={cells[2]} graphOpen={true} /></div>

  <h2 id="arrays">Arrays</h2>
  <p>
    Arrays hold floats. Literals nest into matrices, indexing takes
    comma-separated subscripts (<code>M[i, j]</code>, up to rank 4), <code>zeros</code> allocates,
    <code>len</code> reads the leading dimension. Arithmetic is elementwise — <code>*</code>
    between arrays is the Hadamard product, and an array times a scalar scales. A
    <code>show</code> on a matrix indents its rows under the first one, so it reads as a matrix
    rather than as a label with a ragged list beneath it:
  </p>
  <div class="cellhost"><VivCell client:load idx={3} src={cells[3]} /></div>

  <h2 id="plots">Plots</h2>
  <p>
    A figure is declared in the program. <code>figure(title, xlabel, ylabel)</code> says what the
    chart is, and <code>plot(x, y, "label")</code> adds a series to it. Both are emitted as
    structured records; whatever runs the program (this page, the workbench, a terminal) draws
    them, and the C backend prints the same records as text. Nothing outside the program adds to a figure: the title and
    axis labels you see below are the three strings in the cell, and a program that calls
    <code>plot</code> without declaring its figure first does not compile. Consecutive
    <code>plot</code> calls sharing the same x land in one chart as separate labeled series:
  </p>
  <div class="cellhost">
    <VivCell client:load idx={4} src={cells[4]} />
  </div>

  <h2 id="linear-algebra">Linear algebra</h2>
  <p>
    <code>@</code> is always the matrix product, never elementwise. Alongside it are
    <code>transpose</code> and <code>eig_sym</code>, a Jacobi eigensolver for symmetric matrices
    that fills a values vector (ascending) and a vectors matrix (eigenvectors in columns). The
    cell checks that the trace equals the sum of the eigenvalues:
  </p>
  <div class="cellhost"><VivCell client:load idx={5} src={cells[5]} /></div>

  <h2 id="lambdas">Lambdas, quadrature and exact derivatives</h2>
  <p>
    <code>fn(x) -&gt; expr</code> is a value: assign it, pass it. The higher-order builtins take
    one — <code>simpson</code> and <code>trapezoid</code> integrate, and <code>deriv</code>
    differentiates <em>exactly</em>, by forward-mode autodiff at compile time rather than by
    finite differences. Its vocabulary is arithmetic plus
    <code>exp</code>/<code>sqrt</code>/<code>pow</code>/<code>sin</code>/<code>cos</code>/<code
      >log</code
    >/<code>tan</code>; ask it to differentiate through a <code>def</code> you wrote and it stops with an error that says so:
  </p>
  <div class="cellhost"><VivCell client:load idx={6} src={cells[6]} /></div>

  <h2 id="tests">Tests</h2>
  <p>
    A <code>test</code> block names a claim and asserts it with <code>assert_near</code> — and
    what it prints is <strong>the name you wrote</strong>: one line per test, however many
    assertions are inside. A failure names the test and carries the failing assertion's detail,
    and makes the whole run exit nonzero, so a build script can fail on it:
  </p>
  <div class="cellhost"><VivCell client:load idx={7} src={cells[7]} /></div>

  <h2 id="narration">Progress lines</h2>
  <p>
    <code>narrate "text"</code> prints a progress line, kept separate from data output. A program can
    print "building hamiltonian" or "calculating ERIs" as it reaches each step. Every backend prints it: the interpreter styles the lines, and compiled C prints the
    same <code>narrate:</code> text.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two other kinds of text print nothing. Comments (<code>#</code>) are dropped by the lexer, and
    annotation markers (<code>#|</code> what, <code>#||</code> how, <code>#|||</code> why) are
    comments the compiler ignores but <code>viv doc</code> extracts for code browsers:
  </p>
  <div class="cellhost"><VivCell client:load idx={8} src={cells[8]} /></div>

  <h2 id="parallel">Parallel loops</h2>
  <p>
    <code>parallel for</code> declares a loop parallelizable. The interpreter runs it
    sequentially; the C backend emits an OpenMP pragma, so the compiled program spreads it across
    cores. On a 12-core laptop the Hartree–Fock calculation on a chain of 20 hydrogen atoms
    (<code>hchain_sto3g.viv</code>) goes from 2.67 s on one thread to 0.35 s on twelve:
  </p>
  <div class="cellhost"><VivCell client:load idx={9} src={cells[9]} /></div>

  <h2 id="records">Records</h2>
  <p>
    A <code>record</code> is a named bundle of numbers, arrays and other records. It is built with
    every field named, read with a dot, and passed to and returned from a def like any value.
    Copying a record copies its numbers, while an array field still refers to the original array,
    so bundling a mesh into one value copies no array data. The fields themselves are fixed once
    the record is built: to change one, build a new record. A def that returns several values,
    <code>return a, b</code>, hands back a record the compiler makes for it, and the caller
    unpacks it with <code>lo, hi = extent(v)</code>.
  </p>
  <div class="cellhost"><VivCell client:load idx={10} src={cells[10]} /></div>

  <h2 id="capstone">One Gaussian for the hydrogen atom</h2>
  <p>
    A <code>def</code> with a docstring, <code>deriv</code> and a <code>test</code>, used together
    for the first step of a Hartree–Fock calculation: the best single-Gaussian approximation to the
    hydrogen atom. The energy comes out −0.4244 hartree, and <code>deriv</code> returns zero at the
    analytic optimum, which confirms it is a stationary point of the energy:
  </p>
  <div class="cellhost"><VivCell client:load idx={11} src={cells[11]} /></div>

  <div class="nextprev">
    <span><a href="/docs">Docs by example →</a></span>
    <span><a href="/compare">Python and Viv, side by side →</a></span>
    <span><a href="/reference">The reference tables →</a></span>
  </div>
  <p>
    Or open the <a href="/workbench">workbench</a> and write something of your own — the full
    Hartree–Fock, the neutron star and the FEM solver are all in its examples menu.
  </p>
</Base>
